RULE NUMBER
009

TITLE
Forge: Generate Pulumi module package (module/ only, multi-resource orchestration)

ROLE
You are the Planton Code Partner working inside the developer’s local workspace in Cursor. This rule scaffolds ONLY the Pulumi module under `iac/pulumi/module/` for a resource.

TOOLS
- Proto Readers: reuse readers for `api.proto`, `spec.proto`, `input.proto`, `outputs.proto`.
- Module Writer: `_rules/component/_scripts/pulumi_module_write.py` writes multiple files to the module directory.
- Reference components (live ground truth): `catalog/aws/awsvpc/iac/pulumi/module/`
  and `catalog/aws/awsalb/iac/pulumi/module/` -- canonical multi-resource module layout (main.go controller,
  locals.go, outputs.go, resource_*.go).

SCOPE
- Detect provider and kind.
- Target: `catalog/<provider>/<kindfolder>/iac/pulumi/module/` (the component-root `iac/`)
- Create/update typical files: `main.go`, `locals.go`, `outputs.go`, `resource_*.go` (do not add BUILD.bazel here).

SEQUENTIAL STEPS
1) Read: Use proto readers to derive package paths, `<Kind>`, `<Kind>Spec`, `<Kind>StackInput`, `<Kind>StackOutputs`, and credential type.
2) Draft: Generate content for module files (controller, locals, outputs, resource_*.go) modeled on the reference component `module/` directories. Comment the code to the module-comment bar (`architecture/component.md` §4.1 Code Quality): explain *why*, trade-offs, provider quirks, and non-obvious ordering -- not line-by-line narration. These modules render on the public catalog, so their comments are part of the deliverable.
3) Write: Provide a JSON manifest to the module writer tool:
   - `cat <<'JSON' | python3 _rules/component/_scripts/pulumi_module_write.py --provider <provider> --kindfolder <kindfolder> --stdin --build | cat`
     { "files": [ {"name": "main.go", "content": "..."}, {"name": "locals.go", "content": "..."} ] }
     JSON
   - If build fails, refine content and re-run (≤3).

OUTPUT
- Show created/updated file list and key JSON fields from the writer tool.
- On build failure: note the error and re-run after fixes (≤3).

SUCCESS CRITERIA
- `module/` contains controller, locals, outputs, and resource files reflecting multi-resource orchestration.
- Outputs map to `<Kind>StackOutputs`.
- Module code is richly commented to the module-comment bar (why/trade-offs/provider quirks/ordering, not narration).
- If `--build` used, `go build` succeeds.

NOTES
- Rule 010 handles the entrypoint `iac/pulumi/main.go` and `Pulumi.yaml`.
- Use in-repo patterns (e.g., `awsalb`, `awsrdsinstance`) as guidance; always derive names/types/outputs from the proto files you read.

CLOUD RESOURCE NAMES -- NEVER RELY ON PULUMI AUTO-NAMING
- Always set the resource's own name argument (`Name:`, `BucketName:`,
  `WorkgroupName:`, ...) explicitly from the module's naming basis
  (`metadata.name`). When the name argument is left unset, Pulumi AUTO-NAMES the
  cloud resource by appending a random suffix to the Pulumi resource name -- so
  the same manifest deploys `<name>-<hex>` on Pulumi but `<name>` on Terraform, a
  silent cross-engine identity divergence that no offline gate catches (both
  modules validate and build; only comparing the live resource names reveals it).
- Passing `metadata.name` as the Pulumi RESOURCE name (the second argument to
  `NewX`) does NOT set the cloud name; the resource name is Pulumi-internal.
  Prefer a stable, generic resource name ("table", "workgroup") and carry the
  cloud identity exclusively through the name argument.

EXPORTING OUTPUTS -- APPLYT TYPING IS A DEPLOY-TIME TRAP
- Prefer exporting resource outputs DIRECTLY (`ctx.Export(OpX, created.X)`). Most SDK
  string attributes are `pulumi.StringOutput` and already resolve to `""` when the
  underlying value is absent -- no nil-normalizing transform is needed.
- **NEVER call `ctx.Export` INSIDE an `ApplyT` callback.** Apply callbacks run on
  output-resolution goroutines, and an export registered there writes the SDK's
  shared exports map while the engine's end-of-program stack-output marshaling
  reads it -- a data race that kills the whole program with
  `fatal error: concurrent map read and map write`, timing-dependent and therefore
  FLAKY (live-caught: a per-index export loop inside an ApplyT crashed one deploy
  and passed the next, identical code). `ctx.Export(key, out.ApplyT(...))` is safe --
  it is the export REGISTRATION that must stay on the program goroutine. For
  per-element keys over a computed list, derive the index space from the spec
  (known before the program returns) and register every key synchronously with an
  index-guarded ApplyT value.
- When a real transform IS needed, the `ApplyT` applier's input parameter must match
  the output's element type EXACTLY: `StringOutput` takes `func(string) T`,
  `StringPtrOutput` takes `func(*string) T`. A `func(*string)` applier on a
  `StringOutput` compiles cleanly and panics only at live deploy
  ("applier's first input parameter must be assignable from string, got *string") --
  no offline gate catches it. `go doc` the SDK type to confirm Output vs PtrOutput
  before writing the applier.
- Values nested in computed LIST/POINTER attributes (e.g. a connection endpoint the
  SDK models as `XEndpointArrayOutput`) do not need an ApplyT either: `Index(...)`
  returns the zero value when out of range and `Elem()` dereferences a Ptr output to
  its zero value when nil, so a chain like
  `created.Endpoints.Index(pulumi.Int(0)).Address().Elem()` is a stable, panic-free
  export shape.
- NESTED-BLOCK ARGS: assign the VALUE `XArgs{...}` struct to an `XPtrInput` field
  directly -- the codegen'd value type implements the Ptr interface. Do NOT wrap it
  in the SDK's `XPtr(&XArgs{...})` helper for resource inputs: the wrapper's element
  type trips the engine's input-marshaling assertion ("cannot assign xPtrType to X")
  -- another compiles-clean, panics-only-at-deploy class no offline gate catches.
HANDLING OPTIONAL FIELDS WITH DEFAULTS
- Fields with `(dev.planton.shared.options.default)` are `optional` (pointer types in Go)
- ALWAYS use getter methods: `spec.GetFieldName()` instead of `spec.FieldName`
- **Default materialization is middleware behavior, NOT a wire guarantee.** Stack inputs
  built directly from a manifest (the E2E harness, `planton pulumi up --stack-input`)
  deliver unset optionals as nil, so a bare getter yields the Go zero value. That is fine
  for fields where the provider treats zero as "unset" — but it silently diverges from the
  Terraform module's `optional(type, default)` encoding, and it HARD-FAILS deploy for any
  field the provider range-validates (e.g. an interval that must be >= 5, a threshold in
  1-100: sending 0 is rejected at `pulumi up`, and no offline gate catches it).
- Therefore: **every optional field with a proto default MUST be presence-guarded in the
  Pulumi module**, falling back to the proto-declared default — the same value the
  Terraform module's `optional(type, default)` carries. Never invent a different fallback.
  ```go
  // CORRECT: presence-guard with the proto default (matches TF's optional(number, 15))
  if spec.IntervalInSeconds != nil {
      args.IntervalInSeconds = pulumi.Int(int(spec.GetIntervalInSeconds()))
  } else {
      args.IntervalInSeconds = pulumi.Int(15)
  }

  // WRONG: bare getter — deploys 0 on stack-input paths and fails provider validation
  // args.IntervalInSeconds = pulumi.Int(int(spec.GetIntervalInSeconds()))

  // WRONG: a fallback that differs from the proto default (engine divergence)
  // if l.RunnerGroup == "" { l.RunnerGroup = "some-other-value" }
  ```
- **RequiredWith-paired optionals: gate on the PAIRING field, not just presence.** Some
  provider schemas declare `RequiredWith` between an optional flag and another argument
  (e.g. a boolean that only exists for a VNet-injected shape, paired with the subnet
  argument). For these, presence-guarding is NOT enough: manifest-driven stack-input
  paths materialize proto `(default)` options into concrete values, so an optional bool
  arrives non-nil as `false` and gets SENT — and the provider rejects a SPECIFIED value
  (even `false`) without its pairing argument. Send such fields only when the pairing
  field is also set, and mirror the same conditional in the Terraform module
  (`var.pairing != null ? var.flag : null`). The offline plan does not catch this class
  when the `e2e/manifest.yaml` fixture exercises the paired shape — keep the minimal/unpaired
  shape live-proven or plan-proven too.
- **ONE-WAY / Computed provider flags: send only when true, on BOTH engines.** Some
  provider bools are one-way enables (the service cannot disable them once on) or
  Computed (the service turns them on inherently for certain shapes). For these, a plain
  proto bool's `false` must mean "leave it to the provider", never "disable": send the
  argument only when the spec value is true, and mirror the exact same only-when-true
  posture in the Terraform module (`var.x ? true : null`). Always-sending `false` on one
  engine plans a destructive replacement or fights the service, while the other engine
  silently no-ops — an audit-only divergence no offline gate catches. Document the
  "false means leave it to the provider" contract on the spec field.
- **PROVIDER BUDGETS ON MODULE-DERIVED VALUES: check them in the module, in BOTH engines,
  with one constant and one message.** When a service caps something the module itself
  assembles -- a name it derives from `metadata.name`, a tag set it composes from the
  spec plus the Planton labels -- the manifest author cannot see the final value and a
  spec rule cannot compute it, so the module owns the check: a package-level constant
  naming the measured budget (with the date and the API's own error text in its comment),
  a computation on the FINAL value actually sent (after dedup, after label rendering), and
  an `errors.Errorf` before the resource is created that states the budget, the offending
  total, and the levers the author has (a shorter name or id, fewer entries). Mirror the
  exact same constant and message in the Terraform module as a `lifecycle { precondition }`
  on the resource -- the two are twins and say so in their comments. A budget guessed from
  documentation is not a budget: measure the boundary live (pass at N, fail at N+1) before
  writing the number. Exemplars: the Superset chart's name budget; the DigitalOcean
  database cluster's 255-character combined-tags budget.
- **ENUM WIRE MAPS: map the UNSPECIFIED row to the default wire value whenever
  unspecified is legal.** A Go `map[Enum]string` lookup on a missing key silently
  returns `""`, and `pulumi.String("")` SENDS the empty string — the provider rejects it
  at deploy ("expected X to be one of [...], got ") or, worse, the service
  misinterprets it. No offline gate catches this: `go build`, spec tests, and even the
  Terraform plan all pass (the TF module's `locals.tf` idiom uses an explicit
  `!= null && != ""` fallback, so only the Pulumi engine fails). For every enum field
  where 0/unspecified is legal (a friendly-default enum), either add an explicit
  unspecified row to the wire map carrying the default wire value, or guard the lookup
  and skip the argument when the provider's own default should apply — and mirror
  whichever posture the Terraform module has. Required enums (`not_in: [0]`) don't need
  the row: validation stops unspecified before the module runs.
- PULUMI OUTPUT TYPING: an `ApplyT` applier's input parameter type must match the SDK
  output's element type EXACTLY (a `func(addr *string)` applier on a `StringOutput`
  panics `applier's first input parameter must be assignable from string` at deploy
  time — it compiles clean and no offline gate catches it). Prefer exporting SDK outputs
  directly; reach for `ApplyT` only when genuinely transforming, and check the output's
  `ElementType` (e.g. via `go doc` on the SDK type) before writing the applier.
- See the reference component `module/` directories named in TOOLS for worked examples
